    <section class="section prose seo-section" id="how-to-play" aria-label="How to play Binary Puzzles">
      <h2>How to Play Binary Puzzles</h2>
      <p>Binary puzzles — also known as <strong>Takuzu</strong> — are the friendliest logic grids on the internet: every cell holds exactly one of two symbols, <strong>0 or 1</strong>. Start from the few given digits and fill the rest of the grid using three simple rules:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Equal balance.</strong> Every row and every column must contain the same number of 0s and 1s. A 6×6 row finishes with three of each, an 8×8 row with four of each.</li>
        <li><strong>No triples.</strong> No three identical digits may sit next to each other, horizontally or vertically. Runs like <strong>000</strong> and <strong>111</strong> are always illegal.</li>
        <li><strong>Unique rows and columns.</strong> No two rows and no two columns may be identical — the rule that gives Takuzu puzzles their name.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>On GridPaw, click any empty cell to cycle it between 0 and 1 (click again to clear it back to empty). Prefer typing? Select a cell and press the <strong>0</strong> or <strong>1</strong> key, <strong>Backspace</strong> clears it, and the arrow keys move you around the board. Cells that break a rule get a red tint as you play, and the Hint button (3 per puzzle) drops in the correct digit whenever you are stuck.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose seo-section" aria-label="Takuzu vs Binary Puzzle vs Binairo">
      <h2>What Is Takuzu? Binary Puzzle vs Binairo vs Takuzu</h2>
      <p>The binary puzzle has travelled under several names, and they all describe the <strong>exact same game</strong>. It was invented in <strong>Belgium in 2009</strong> and quickly caught on across Europe: in the Netherlands it is best known as <strong>Binairo</strong> (from "binary zero"), while most puzzle apps and newspapers elsewhere publish it as <strong>Takuzu</strong>, the Japanese-themed name that suits the puzzle's elegant minimalism.</p>
      <p>Add "binary puzzle" and "binario" to the list and you have found every version of the same rules — if you have solved one, you have solved them all. At GridPaw we call the game <strong>Binary Puzzle</strong> because it is the clearest, most searchable name, but you will regularly see the identical grid published elsewhere as Takuzu or Binairo. Same 0s, same 1s, same three rules — just a different sticker on the box.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose seo-section" aria-label="Binary Puzzle vs Sudoku">
      <h2>Binary Puzzle vs Sudoku</h2>
      <p><strong>Sudoku</strong> and the <strong>binary puzzle</strong> both belong to the single-solution logic family, but they are almost opposites in flavour. Sudoku works with nine digits and a box rule: each 3×3 region must contain 1 through 9 exactly once, so solving is about uniqueness — every digit appears exactly once in each row, column and box. A binary puzzle collapses everything to <strong>two symbols</strong> and swaps uniqueness for <strong>balance</strong>: each row and column needs an equal count of 0s and 1s, and the only prohibition is three-of-a-kind in a row.</p>
      <p>Where Sudoku asks you to cross-hatch candidates across nine digits, a binary puzzle takes the same discipline — scan, spot the pattern, eliminate — and distils it to its bare essence. That minimalism makes it the perfect gateway puzzle: kids grasp it in seconds, adults still find the 10×10 grids properly demanding, and a session fits neatly into a coffee break.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose seo-section" id="tips" aria-label="Binary puzzle tips and strategy">
      <h2>Binary Puzzle Tips and Strategy</h2>
      <p>Three techniques unlock almost every GridPaw binary puzzle:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>The pairs rule.</strong> Two identical digits side by side — <strong>00</strong> or <strong>11</strong> — force the cell on each outside flank to be the opposite digit. The same trick works across one gap: <strong>0_0</strong> forces a 1 into the middle cell.</li>
        <li><strong>Count your rows and columns.</strong> In a 6×6 grid every row and column must end with exactly three 0s and three 1s. When a row already contains three of one digit, every remaining empty cell in that row must be the other digit — fill them all confidently, then do the same sweep for columns.</li>
        <li><strong>The edge start rule.</strong> Study the first cells of any row or column. A line that begins <strong>000</strong> is already illegal, a line beginning <strong>00</strong> forces its third cell to 1, and a line beginning <strong>0_0</strong> forces the middle cell to 1. These forced placements cascade down the whole line.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Then finish with the <strong>Takuzu rule</strong>: on the larger grids, once most rows are complete, scan for two rows that look identical — they are not allowed, so one digit somewhere in the pair has to flip, and the same logic applies to columns. On a nearly full board that last rule is usually the key that unlocks the final cells. Every GridPaw puzzle is generated with exactly one solution, so pure logic always gets you there — the three Hints are just a safety net.</p>
    </section>

